    "17592186405205": {
      "course/code": "MTH 256",
      "course/description": "Applied Differential Equations introduces methods for describing and solving problems in which an unknown function is related to its derivatives. The course focuses on ordinary differential equations and applications appropriate to science and engineering, particularly electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators. Coverage includes first-order linear and selected nonlinear equations, second-order linear equations, and introductory work with higher-order equations and Laplace transforms.",
      "course/id": {
        "$uuid": "fa274c9b-ca65-5fde-b8ed-49681698e235"
      },
      "course/outcomes": [
        17592186406528,
        17592186406529,
        17592186406530,
        17592186406531,
        17592186406532,
        17592186406533,
        17592186406534,
        17592186406535,
        17592186406536
      ],
      "course/overview": "The archived syllabus begins with mathematical modeling and the classification of differential equations, then develops methods for first-order and second-order equations. This organization connects the structure of an equation with an appropriate solution approach. Applications give meaning to the unknown functions and their derivatives rather than treating equations as purely formal expressions.\n\nLaplace transforms provide an additional approach to solving differential equations, while introductory higher-order work extends the range of equations considered. Electrical circuits and mechanical oscillators serve as central application settings. The published description also identifies familiarity with complex numbers and Euler’s identities as relevant background.",
      "course/school": "Oregon State University",
      "course/title": "Applied Differential Equations",
      "db/id": 17592186405205
    },
